+
+This section defines the Multi-Period Vehicle Routing Problem with Profits
+(MPVRPP) that the benchmark addresses. It extends the single-period Smart
+Waste Collection Routing Problem of Ramos et al.~\cite{RAMOS2018146} from one
+decision period to a horizon $T=\{1,\dots,\tau\}$, and it does so on the
+road network as it is actually driven: every distance $d_{ij}$ is a directed
+road kilometre, priced in the direction travelled.
+The source model is stated on an undirected network; the formulation below is
+written on directed arcs, which is both what the benchmark data provides and
+what the implemented constructors solve.
+
+\subsection{Problem Description}\label{sec:prob-desc}
+
+A single depot serves a set $I_b=\{1,\dots,n\}$ of sensor-equipped bins over
+the periods of a planning horizon. Every morning the sensors transmit the
+fill level of each bin, converted from volume to mass by the waste density
+$B$, producing the reading $w_i$; a daily accumulation $a_i$ (kg per period)
+is estimated from the sensor history. Identical vehicles of capacity $Q$
+leave the depot, collect, and return to it. Every kilogram collected earns
+$R$ and every kilometre driven costs $C$; a per-vehicle charge $\Omega$
+penalises fleet usage in the reference model.
+
+The operational evidence motivating dynamic collection is the case recorded
+by Ramos et al.~\cite{RAMOS2018146}: on a 226-bin, 30-day deployment, about
+10\% of the bins visited on a route were empty (peaking at 38\% on one day),
+about 66\% of recorded fills were at or below half capacity, vehicles ran at
+57\% utilisation, and the collection ratio stood at 15.5\,kg/km. Sensor
+information changes the question from \emph{which fixed route to repeat} to
+two coupled decisions: \emph{which} bins to visit, if any, and \emph{in what
+sequence}, on each day and for each vehicle. The service level is expressed
+by a maximum overflow threshold $\psi$, the fill fraction of $E_i$ at which a
+bin's continued overflow becomes unacceptable and a visit is forced.
+
+\subsection{Assumptions}\label{sec:prob-assumptions}
+
+The formulation inherits the following from the reference
+model~\cite{RAMOS2018146}: (A1) a homogeneous fleet of capacity $Q$ based at
+a single depot; (A2) a complete network with one cost per directed arc (the
+source model is undirected, with Euclidean distances adjusted by a correction
+factor in its case study); (A3) transport cost linear in distance; (A4) no
+time windows, shift limits, or intermediate disposal visits; (A5) at most one
+route per vehicle per period; (A6) a single waste stream; (A7) a constant
+density $B$ common to all bins; (A8) the reading $w_i$ is known exactly at
+the start of each period --- the only uncertainty is in the accumulation,
+which enters the model as an expected value; (A9) a visit empties the bin;
+(A10) service times are not modelled. The multi-period extension adds:
+(A11) the model is deterministic --- it knows the state evolves but assumes
+it evolves exactly as expected (the simulator of
+Sect.~\ref{sec:protocol} replaces $a_i$ by independent draws); and (A12) the
+horizon is finite and the stock left after period $\tau$ carries a residual
+value $\rho$ per kilogram ($\rho = 0$ throughout this paper). Two further
+assumptions support the collection bound $\bar U_i$ used below: the initial
+reading satisfies $w_i \le \psi E_i + \max_{t\in T} a_i^{t}$ (in particular
+$w_i \le E_i$ suffices when $\psi = 1$, as in all experiments), and the
+force-visit rule of \eqref{eq:force} is in force.
+
