+\subsection{The Single-Period Source Model}\label{sec:swcrp}
+
+Ramos et al.~\cite{RAMOS2018146} select bins and routes jointly by making the
+visit binary $g_i$ first-class and charging a per-vehicle penalty $\Omega$.
+Their routing core is the two-commodity network flow formulation of Baldacci
+et al.~\cite{Baldacci2004TwoCommodity}: a load commodity and a residual
+(empty-space) commodity travel the network simultaneously, and their coupling
+$y_{ij} + y_{ji} = Q\,x_{ij}$ enforces vehicle capacity and eliminates
+sub-tours in a single constraint family, replacing the separate capacity and
+sub-tour-elimination families of compact models. The single-period objective
+trades collection revenue against travel cost and the vehicle charge:
+\[
+    \max\; P = R \sum_{i \in I_b} w_i g_i
+    - \Bigl( C \sum_{i \in I} \sum_{j \neq i} x_{ij}\, d_{ij} + k\,\Omega \Bigr),
+\]
+subject to the flow core, conditional degree constraints (two edges at a
+visited bin, none at an unvisited one), and a service level consisting of two
+devices: a share $\delta$ of bins may be left in overflow, enforced by
+counting the bins whose end-of-day content will reach capacity,
+$H = \bigl|\{i \in I_b : w_i + a_i \ge E_i\}\bigr|$, and capping the number of
+them left uncollected; and the threshold rule $g_i = 1$ for every bin with
+$w_i \ge \psi E_i$, the only mechanism that introduces memory between days.
+Two readings of the printed model deserve note: the count cap is consistent
+with the published scenario results only in the $\ge$ direction, and the
+right-hand side of the degree constraint reads $2g_j$ at the quantified node
+$j$; both are the present authors' critical readings, not the source text.
+Operationally, the source approach runs this model day by day: a trigger
+defers the route while $H$ stays within the allowed share, and the state is
+carried between executions outside the optimisation.
+
+This paper's problem definition keeps the visit binary, the per-vehicle
+charge, and the threshold rule. It does not adopt the share-of-bins service
+level: the multi-period model below records overflows at capacity but neither
+caps their number nor triggers on a counting set, so that no share parameter
+appears in the problem and the temporal trade-off is carried entirely by the
+state and the objective.
+
