+\paragraph{Objective.}
+The MPVRPP maximises the profit accumulated over the horizon plus the value
+of the stock left in the bins:
+\begin{equation}
+    \max\; Z = \sum_{t \in T}\Biggl[\, R \sum_{i \in I_b} q_i^{t}
+    - C \sum_{i \in I} \sum_{j \neq i} d_{ij}\, x_{ij}^{t}
+    - \Omega\, k^{t} \Biggr]
+    + \rho \sum_{i \in I_b} w_i^{\tau+1},
+    \label{eq:mpvrpp}
+\end{equation}
+subject to \eqref{eq:overflow}--\eqref{eq:fleet} and the variable domains of
+Table~\ref{tab:notation}. A period with no route is represented by
+$k^{t} = 0$, $g_i^{t} = 0$, and $x_{ij}^{t} = 0$ for all $i,j$, so the
+decision not to dispatch is part of the model rather than an external rule.
+
+\paragraph{Size and limitations.}
+The model grows linearly in $\tau$: a directed instance on $n$ bins has
+$(n{+}2)(n{+}1)$ arc binaries per period --- $123{,}552$ at $n = 350$, or
+about $3.7 \times 10^{6}$ over a 30-period horizon before the flow variables
+are counted --- which is why the full model is a reference object rather than
+a solution method at the benchmark sizes, and why the evaluation proceeds by
+simulation over decomposed policies. Like the single-period source, the
+reference model does not track lost mass: content above $E_i$ transits into
+$w_i^{t+1}$ and is recorded by \eqref{eq:overflow}, but no mass is discarded
+and no cleanup cost is charged. The simulator applies the complementary
+convention: the displayed level is capped at $E_i$ and the discarded mass
+$\ell_i^{t}$ is recorded, as defined in Sect.~\ref{sec:protocol}.
