+
+The central reading is that the optimum collects \emph{less} mass and earns
+\emph{more} money: the profit does not come from collecting more, but from
+collecting when each kilogram pays a larger share of the route --- exactly
+the kg/km ratio that the reference article uses as its efficiency indicator.
+With $\rho = 0$ part of the horizon advantage is deferral (115\,kg of stock
+is retained versus none); the $\rho = R$ column prices retained mass at the
+revenue rate, and the advantage stands, so it is not an artefact of the
+finite horizon. Four bins and three periods illustrate the mechanism; they
+do not measure the gain on a real network.
